# The funnel home page is now served flat at the root (see tools/build-root-index.py).
# These two paths are its old location. Anything that still points there - nurture
# emails, older ads, bookmarks, the in-site nav links - lands on the root instead of
# serving a second copy of the same page at a second URL.
#
# Only the home page moved. Every other /funnel-build/ path (booking, apply, the SEO
# pages, images, shared.css, shared.js) is untouched and still served from where it
# has always been, so nothing else on the site changes.
#
# Cloudflare Pages carries the query string across a redirect: verified 8/6 against
# the live host, /funnel-build/booking.html?gclid=... kept its gclid through the 308.

/funnel-build/index.html    /    301
/funnel-build/              /    301

# ONE confirmation page for the whole site.
#
# Both arms now land on /optin/thank-you.html after a booking: the opt-in arm
# always did, and the main arm (root -> apply -> booking) was moved onto it in
# shared.js. It is the richer page - add-to-calendar, the pre-call video, the
# eight answer videos - and having a single one means the Schedule pixel, the
# copy and the pre-call videos only ever have to be right in one place.
#
# The page stays where it is rather than being copied to the root because every
# asset it references (funnel.css, styles.css, main.js, funnel.js, the images,
# and its own privacy/terms links) is relative to /optin/. Copying the file up
# would silently break all of them: this host answers 200 with the landing page
# for any path that does not exist, so a missing stylesheet does not 404, it
# quietly returns HTML and the page renders unstyled.
#
# /thank-you is the clean public URL for it. Before this rule it was one of
# those silent 200s and served the landing page to anyone who tried it.
/thank-you                  /optin/thank-you.html    301
/funnel-build/booked.html   /optin/thank-you.html    301
